Ja, absolut – wir können im Go-Interface zwei ergänzende GPT-Ingestions-Pipelines bereitstellen:

1. **GPT-via-API-Module**  
2. **GPT-via-Link-Module**  

und dazu **use-case-Interface-Components** in React, die typische Mandala-Workflows (z. B. Sigil-Generierung, ToDo-Parsing, SocialGood-Matching) per Knopfdruck oder halbautomatisch anstoßen.

---

## 1. Architektur­überblick

```plaintext
go-bridge/
├── pkg/
│   ├── gpt/
│   │   ├── api_client.go         # GPT-via-API
│   │   └── link_processor.go     # GPT-via-Link
│   └── usecases/                 # Go-Layer für standardisierte Workflows
│       ├── sigil_generator.go
│       ├── todo_parser.go
│       └── socialgood_matcher.go
└── cmd/
    └── mandala-gpt.go            # CLI-Entrypoint für GPT-Workflows
```

### 1.1 `pkg/gpt/api_client.go`

```go
package gpt

import (
  "context"
  "encoding/json"
  "net/http"
  "bytes"
  "fmt"
)

type GPTClient struct {
  Endpoint string
  APIKey   string
}

func NewClient(endpoint, apiKey string) *GPTClient {
  return &GPTClient{Endpoint: endpoint, APIKey: apiKey}
}

func (c *GPTClient) Ask(ctx context.Context, prompt string) (string, error) {
  body := map[string]string{"prompt": prompt}
  b, _ := json.Marshal(body)
  req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, "POST", c.Endpoint, bytes.NewReader(b))
  req.Header.Set("Authorization", "Bearer "+c.APIKey)
  req.Header.Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
  res, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
  if err != nil { return "", err }
  defer res.Body.Close()
  var resp struct{ Text string }
  if err := json.NewDecoder(res.Body).Decode(&resp); err != nil {
    return "", err
  }
  return resp.Text, nil
}
```

### 1.2 `pkg/gpt/link_processor.go`

```go
package gpt

import (
  "context"
  "io"
  "net/http"
  "regexp"
)

// Fetches the content at the link, strips HTML, then feeds to GPT API
func (c *GPTClient) ProcessLink(ctx context.Context, url string) (string, error) {
  // 1. Fetch
  resp, err := http.Get(url)
  if err != nil { return "", err }
  defer resp.Body.Close()
  data, _ := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
  // 2. Strip HTML tags (very simple example)
  re := regexp.MustCompile("<[^>]*>")
  text := re.ReplaceAllString(string(data), "")
  // 3. Truncate to safe length
  if len(text) > 2000 {
    text = text[:2000]
  }
  // 4. Ask GPT
  return c.Ask(ctx, text)
}
```

